<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Pereslavl-Zalessky</h1><p class="st-meta">1175 CE – 1302 CE (approx.) · capital Pereslavl-Zalessky</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An appanage principality on Lake Pleshcheyevo, birthplace of Alexander Nevsky, bequeathed to Moscow by its last prince in 1302.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuri-dolgoruky">Yuri Dolgoruky</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pereslavl-zalessky">Pereslavl-Zalessky</a>, Pereslavl beyond the forests, in 1152 on the shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-pleshcheyevo">Lake Pleshcheyevo</a>, naming it after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pereiaslav">Pereyaslavl</a> in the south. He began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transfiguration-cathedral-pereslavl-zalessky">Transfiguration Cathedral</a> there, one of the oldest white-stone churches in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrei-bogolyubsky">Andrei Bogolyubsky</a> it became a separate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/vladimir-suzdal">Vladimir-Suzdal</a> lands.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pereslavl-zalessky">Pereslavl</a> was a fortified town with earthen ramparts that guarded the route from Vladimir to the north. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-ii-of-vladimir">Yaroslav Vsevolodovich</a> held it from 1212, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-nevsky">Alexander Nevsky</a> was born there around 1220. Its princes were close kin of the grand princes of Vladimir, and the town played a part in their many succession struggles.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The last prince, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-dmitrievich-of-pereslavl">Ivan Dmitrievich</a>, a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-nevsky">Alexander Nevsky</a>, died childless in 1302 and left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pereslavl-zalessky">Pereslavl</a> to his uncle Daniel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. This bequest was an early step in the growth of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> later built his first toy fleet on the lake.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="principality, 1175 CE – 1302 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">principality</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:83.08%;width:2.53%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1175 CE – 1302 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Pereslavl-Zalessky</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Yaroslav Vsevolodovich, Alexander Nevsky, Ivan Dmitrievich</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old East Slavic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Russia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Pereslavl Principality</dd></dl></aside></div>
